+---
+id: dl-001-task-ids-are-a-sequential-counter-not-random-or-clock-based
+type: decision-log
+title: "Task ids are a sequential counter, not random or clock-based"
+status: pending
+---
+
+## Context
+
+Tasks needed a stable identifier that survives a rename. The usual ways to mint one — `crypto.randomUUID()`,
+a random string, or a timestamp-derived id — all read either randomness or the clock. The
+`r3-no-clock-or-randomness` directive forbids both under `src/domain/`, where the task list lives.
+
+## Decision
+
+Each `TaskList` keeps a private counter (`nextId`, starting at 1) and assigns `String(nextId++)` as the
+id of every task `add` creates. The counter lives in the closure created by `createTaskList()`, so ids are
+unique within one list, assigned in insertion order, and require no external input. `createTask` in
+`src/domain/task.ts` takes the id as a parameter rather than generating it, keeping that function pure.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Ids are deterministic and reproducible given the same sequence of `add` calls — easy to test, and
+  compliant with `r3-no-clock-or-randomness`.
+- Ids are only unique within a single `TaskList` instance, not globally; nothing in the app currently
+  needs global uniqueness (persistence, merging two lists) — if that need appears, the id scheme will
+  need revisiting.
+- Removing and re-adding a task never reuses an id, since the counter never decreases or resets.
